\par
\fs30\b 6. Role and permission matrix\b0\fs24\par
\par
| Capability | Superadmin | General admin |\par
|---|---:|---:|\par
| View dashboard and content | Yes | Yes |\par
| View contact forms and applications | Yes | Yes |\par
| Reply to contact/job enquiries | Yes | Yes |\par
| Update approved application/interview statuses | Yes | Yes |\par
| Create or edit public content | Yes | No |\par
| Delete public content | Yes | No |\par
| Upload or remove hero/gallery/content files | Yes | No |\par
| Manage academic programmes | Yes | No |\par
| Manage users and roles | Yes | No |\par
| Change global settings | Yes | No |\par
| Read activity logs | Yes | Yes |\par
\par
The deployed Firestore and Storage rules are the final authority. Hiding a button in the interface is not a security boundary; Firebase rules enforce the restriction.\par
\par
\fs30\b 7. Managing dynamic content\b0\fs24\par
\par
\fs26\b General publishing process\b0\fs24\par
\par
For a superadmin:\par
\par
1. Open the relevant management page.\par
2. Review existing records before adding a duplicate.\par
3. Select **Add**, **Edit** or the relevant action.\par
4. Enter concise, accurate content.\par
5. Set the display order where available. Lower numbers appear first.\par
6. Upload an appropriately sized image if required.\par
7. Save or publish.\par
8. Open the public page in a new tab and verify the result on desktop and mobile.\par
\par
\fs26\b Display order\b0\fs24\par
\par
Use numeric ordering to control homepage and listing sequence. Recommended values are `1`, `2`, `3`, and so on. Avoid duplicate numbers when a clear sequence matters.\par
\par
Ordering applies particularly to:\par
\par
â€¢ Academic programmes.\par
â€¢ Hero images.\par
â€¢ Gallery images.\par
â€¢ FAQs.\par
â€¢ Testimonials.\par
â€¢ Leadership profiles.\par
â€¢ School capabilities.\par
â€¢ News and events where supported.\par
\par
\fs26\b Homepage previews\b0\fs24\par
\par
Dynamic homepage sections should show a short preview, not the full record. The visitor selects **Read More**, **View programme**, **Explore**, or the relevant card to open the complete page or detail view.\par
\par
When preparing content for a homepage card:\par
\par
â€¢ Begin with a clear title or opening sentence.\par
â€¢ Put the most important benefit in the first two or three sentences.\par
â€¢ Keep long curriculum, policy or achievement details on the full page.\par
â€¢ Confirm the detail link opens the correct record.\par
\par
\fs30\b 8. Markdown content standard\b0\fs24\par
\par
Markdown is supported in descriptions, biographies, programme content, news, FAQs and other rich-text fields.\par
\par
\fs26\b Recommended syntax\b0\fs24\par
\par
```markdown\par
\fs30\b Programme Overview\b0\fs24\par
\par
The **CrÃ¨che and Nursery programme** supports safe, caring and stimulating early learning.\par
\par
\fs26\b Learning Areas\b0\fs24\par
\par
â€¢ Early language and communication\par
â€¢ Number awareness and basic mathematics\par
â€¢ Creative arts, music and movement\par
\par
Read more about the [admissions process](/admissions).\par
```\par
\par
\fs26\b Content rules\b0\fs24\par
\par
â€¢ Use one `##` heading for a major subsection.\par
â€¢ Use `###` for a subsection under it.\par
â€¢ Use short paragraphs of two to four sentences.\par
â€¢ Use bullet lists for subjects, requirements and features.\par
â€¢ Use bold only for important terms.\par
â€¢ Do not paste raw HTML unless specifically supported by the field.\par
â€¢ Do not place confidential information, passwords or private student records in public content.\par
â€¢ Preview the result after saving; malformed Markdown can affect readability.\par
\par
\fs30\b 9. Image and file standards\b0\fs24\par
\par
\fs26\b Hero images\b0\fs24\par
\par
â€¢ Recommended format: WebP or optimized JPG.\par
â€¢ Maximum target size: approximately 200â€“300 KB.\par
â€¢ Use a wide composition suitable for desktop and mobile crops.\par
â€¢ Keep important faces, logos and text away from extreme edges.\par
â€¢ Use the numeric order field to control the carousel.\par
\par
\fs26\b Gallery images\b0\fs24\par
\par
â€¢ Recommended format: WebP.\par
â€¢ Target size: approximately 120â€“180 KB.\par
â€¢ Use descriptive titles and categories.\par
â€¢ Reuse an existing hero image through the gallery selector when appropriate instead of uploading a duplicate.\par
\par
\fs26\b Documents\b0\fs24\par
\par
â€¢ Use clear filenames without sensitive personal information.\par
â€¢ Upload only approved school documents.\par
â€¢ Confirm the public download link after publishing.\par
\par
\fs26\b Image quality checklist\b0\fs24\par
\par
Before upload, confirm:\par
\par
1. The image is sharp and correctly oriented.\par
2. The file is within the size limit.\par
3. The image does not contain private information.\par
4. The composition works in a responsive container.\par
5. The image is not already published elsewhere unless reuse is intentional.\par
\par
\fs30\b 10. Common operational workflows\b0\fs24\par
\par
\fs26\b Publish a news article\b0\fs24\par
\par
1. Open `/admin/blog` or the news management route.\par
2. Add a clear title.\par
3. Write the article in Markdown.\par
4. Add an optimized image if required.\par
5. Publish and confirm the homepage preview.\par
6. Open the full article and verify Markdown headings, lists and links.\par
\par
\fs26\b Publish an academic programme\b0\fs24\par
\par
1. Open `/admin/manage-services` or `/admin/services`.\par
2. Add the programme title and display order.\par
3. Enter a concise description for previews.\par
4. Add curriculum, assessment and timetable content in Markdown.\par
5. Upload the programme image.\par
6. Save and open `/academics/\{programme-slug\}`.\par
\par
\fs26\b Update About Us\b0\fs24\par
\par
1. Open `/admin/about`.\par
2. Update only the relevant section: introduction, history, vision, mission, values, achievements, philosophy or leadership message.\par
3. Use Markdown headings and lists for long sections.\par
4. Add or replace proprietor/headteacher images when authorised.\par
5. Save and verify `/about` on a large and small screen.\par
\par
\fs26\b Add a staff or leadership profile\b0\fs24\par
\par
1. Open `/admin/leadership`.\par
2. Enter the personâ€™s name, role, display order and biography.\par
3. Upload a clear professional image.\par
4. Keep the biography factual and suitable for public publication.\par
5. Verify the About preview and the full `/staff` profile.\par
\par
\fs26\b Review a contact message\b0\fs24\par
\par
1. Open `/admin/forms`.\par
2. Read the enquiry and confirm the senderâ€™s email.\par
3. Select **Reply**.\par
4. Write a professional response.\par
5. Send/save the reply.\par
\par
\fs26\b Review an admission application\b0\fs24\par
\par
1. Open `/admin/admissions`.\par
2. Review the learner, guardian, programme and contact information.\par
3. Update the status only when the school has completed the relevant step.\par
4. Open **Manage files** when authorised documents must be reviewed.\par
5. Keep personal data confidential.\par
\par
\fs26\b Review a job application\b0\fs24\par
\par
1. Open `/admin/jobs` and select the vacancy applications.\par
2. Select **View Details**.\par
3. Review applicant details, cover message and CV/PDF.\par
4. Use the reply workflow for approved communication.\par
5. Do not download or share applicant information outside authorised school processes.\par
\par
\fs30\b 11. Security and data protection\b0\fs24\par
\par
â€¢ Never share administrator passwords.\par
â€¢ Use individual Firebase accounts rather than shared credentials.\par
â€¢ Sign out after using a shared computer.\par
â€¢ Use `superadmin` only for tasks that require elevated authority.\par
â€¢ Do not upload identity documents to public content folders.\par
â€¢ Do not place API secrets, service-account keys or private tokens in the frontend repository.\par
â€¢ Review access whenever a staff member leaves or changes responsibility.\par
â€¢ Treat admissions, job applications, contact messages and alumni records as confidential.\par
â€¢ Report unexpected permission errors rather than weakening Firebase rules.\par
\par
\fs30\b 12. Troubleshooting\b0\fs24\par
\par
\fs26\b A page is blank\b0\fs24\par
\par
Refresh the page, return to the homepage and navigate again. If the issue affects one content card, check that its Firestore record contains the expected title and content fields.\par
\par
\fs26\b A route shows 404\b0\fs24\par
\par
Open the main domain first, then navigate through the application. Firebase Hosting is configured for single-page application rewrites; a stale browser cache can also show an old route.\par
\par
\fs26\b An image does not display\b0\fs24\par
\par
Check that the record contains a valid `imageUrl` or `iconUrl`, that the file upload completed, and that the user has the required role. Confirm the Storage path matches the featureâ€™s approved folder.\par
\par
\fs26\b â€œMissing or insufficient permissionsâ€\b0\fs24\par
\par
This normally means a general admin attempted a superadmin-only write, or the Authentication UID does not have a matching Firestore user profile. Confirm the user document and use the correct role.\par
\par
\fs26\b Markdown appears as plain text\b0\fs24\par
\par
Check that the content is entered in a Markdown-enabled field and that headings use `##` or `###` followed by a space. Save, refresh and inspect the full detail page.\par
\par
\fs26\b A homepage card shows too much text\b0\fs24\par
\par
The source content is probably missing a preview boundary or is being rendered by an outdated build. Confirm the public preview component, rebuild and deploy only after verification.\par
\par
\fs26\b Mobile layout is difficult to use\b0\fs24\par
\par
Test both portrait and landscape orientations. Confirm that the browser is not zoomed, the menu can scroll, and the latest deployed build is loaded after a hard refresh.\par
\par
\fs30\b 13. Local testing and release checklist\b0\fs24\par
\par
Before a release:\par
\par
1. Run the local app with `npm start`.\par
2. Test homepage, About, Academics, Admissions, Jobs, Contact and admin login.\par
3. Test mobile portrait, mobile landscape, tablet, laptop and desktop.\par
4. Test at least one form submission and one admin response workflow.\par
5. Confirm images and Markdown render correctly.\par
6. Run the production build:\par
\par
```powershell\par
$env:GENERATE_SOURCEMAP='false'; npm run build\par
```\par
\par
7. Review `git status` and confirm only intended changes exist.\par
8. Deploy hosting only after the build succeeds:\par
\par
```powershell\par
firebase use dankamf-eduplex\par
firebase deploy --only hosting\par
```\par
\par
9. Deploy rules separately and review their security impact:\par
\par
```powershell\par
firebase deploy --only firestore:rules,storage\par
```\par
\par
10. Verify the live website after deployment using a private/incognito browser window.\par
\par
\fs30\b 14. Support information\b0\fs24\par
\par
For a technical incident, record:\par
\par
â€¢ The exact URL.\par
â€¢ The signed-in role.\par
â€¢ The action being performed.\par
â€¢ The exact error message.\par
â€¢ Device, browser and orientation.\par
â€¢ Whether the issue occurs locally, in production or both.\par
â€¢ The approximate time of the incident.\par
\par
This information makes debugging faster and prevents unnecessary changes to security rules or production data.\par
